WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:05.680
In this 1995 episode of The Simpsons, Homer
walks with Marge through downtown Springfield

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.680
and says

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:07.680
Careful now.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.410
These are dangerous streets for us upper-lower-middle-class
types

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:15.200
This is Homer’s first admission to being
"middle-class"

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.520
For the rest of us, this always seemed obvious.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.430
He lives in a modest home in the suburbs with
his wife and three children.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:25.660
He’s not a college graduate and his job
appears to require a minimal amount of technical

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:26.660
training.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.820
This is all confirmed when we get a tight
shot of his paycheck in season 7.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.250
Hey, how come my pay is so low?

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:41.360
According to this stub, Homer receives a pre-tax,
weekly pay of $479.60 works out to $11.99

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.630
an hour.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:48.010
So, he’s looking at an annual salary of
$24,395.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:51.440
Adjusted for inflation, that’s $37,416 per
year.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:52.440
What?

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.210
This is an outrage!

00:00:54.210 --> 00:01:01.680
There are a lot of fans who think the show
is based on the real life town of Springfield,

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:02.680
Oregon.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:06.880
And if we look at Homer's salary there it
places him pretty comfortably in the lower

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.649
middle class income bracket.

00:01:08.649 --> 00:01:13.999
Of course, this is all based on one job as
a safety inspector at the Springfield Nuclear

00:01:13.999 --> 00:01:14.999
Power Plant.

00:01:14.999 --> 00:01:21.259
But Homer has had over 191 jobs in 27 seasons
and they’ve placed him across the entire

00:01:21.259 --> 00:01:22.259
economic spectrum.

00:01:22.259 --> 00:01:24.590
You know, I've had a lot of jobs.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:29.409
Boxer, mascot, astronaut, imitation Krusty,
baby proofer, trucker, hippie, plow driver,

00:01:29.409 --> 00:01:34.850
food critic, conceptual artist, grease salesman,
carnie, mayor, grifter, bodyguard for the

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:39.380
mayor, garbage commissioner, mountain climber,
farmer, inventor, Smithers, Poochie, celebrity

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:43.719
assistant, power plant worker, fortune cookie
writer, beer baron, Kwik-E Mart clerk, homophobe

00:01:43.719 --> 00:01:44.779
and missionary.

00:01:44.779 --> 00:01:48.460
But protecting Springfield, that gives me
the best feeling of all.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:53.220
Some of these jobs were literally impossible
to determine salaries for — like “beer

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.240
smuggler,” or “the grim reaper.”

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:00.169
There were also some seasonal jobs (“mall
santa”), and those had to be excluded.

00:02:00.169 --> 00:02:04.929
In the end, I narrowed Homer’s resume down
to 100 jobs, then looked up the average salary

00:02:04.929 --> 00:02:05.929
for each one.

00:02:05.929 --> 00:02:07.479
Here’s what we found.

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:10.939
Three of Homer’s 10 highest-paying jobs
have been at the power plant.

00:02:10.939 --> 00:02:16.140
In season 13, he tried his hand as the plant’s
executive VP before momentarily taking over

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.980
Mr. Burns’s post as CEO the following season.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.540
Dad please you're the head of a major corporation.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.040
You're right.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.510
Three years later, he served as the facility’s
manager.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:28.909
Dohhhhhhhhh.

00:02:28.909 --> 00:02:30.319
Uhhhmmmm.

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:31.719
Uhhhh.

00:02:31.719 --> 00:02:33.129
Duhhhhhhh.

00:02:33.129 --> 00:02:36.560
Around half of Homer’s jobs place him in
the middle class.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.310
And his least lucrative jobs were pretty odd.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:44.640
He was a mascot, a carny, moonshine taste
tester, a cannonball performance artist, and

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.969
a walking billboard.

00:02:45.969 --> 00:02:49.360
What are those doing there?!

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.800
Earning us a hundred bucks a week that's what.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:57.680
I plotted out Homer’s hypothetical job salaries
in a linear order, by episode number.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:05.420
And over the course of 597 episodes — from
1989 to 2016 — it’s clear that he hasn’t

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:06.670
really ascended economically.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:11.709
Estelle: Despite a few successes here and
there, he has stagnated and that makes him

00:03:11.709 --> 00:03:15.109
just like the actual American middle class.

00:03:15.109 --> 00:03:21.879
Until the 1970s, the income of the average
American family grew alongside national economic

00:03:21.879 --> 00:03:23.250
productivity.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:28.010
Since then, wages have stagnated, and have
failed to keep up with inflation.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:32.439
Economists refer to this as the ‘middle-class
squeeze’.

00:03:32.439 --> 00:03:39.000
Homer’s median income has never surpassed
the median income in the United States.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:45.329
Despite brief forays into the 1 percent, Homer
remains a paradigm of middle-class America:

00:03:45.329 --> 00:04:04.989
Three decades later, he’s right where he
started.

